    "body": "The service worker needs to know what kind of type is being requested... a service worker could potentially be used as a mean to polyfill the type being asked for if the supported type ain't supported natively by the browser itself. \n\n\n```js\nimport spriteBlob from \"./sprite.png\" with { type: \"blob\" };\n```\n```js\nonfetch = evt => {\n  if (evt.request.destination === 'script' && (evt.withType === 'blob' || evt.withType === 'text')) {\n    const polyfill = `export default await fetch(\n       \"${evt.request.url}\"\n    ).then(res => res.${evt.withType}())`\n\n    const response = new Response(polyfill, { \n      headers: { \"content-type\": \"text/javascript\" } \n    })\n\n    evt.respondWith(response)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nthe 2nd best would just be to use own url search params and polyfill it that way and just discard the with-type alltogheter\n```js\nimport spriteBlob from \"./sprite.png?type=blob\";\n```",
